{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-22",
              "name": "Path traversal in pack-deploy endpoints allows reading files outside the industry-packs tree",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Path traversal in pack-deploy endpoints allows reading files outside the industry-packs tree"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The `packId` and `scopeDirName` parameters of the pack-deploy endpoints (`GET /api/packs/onboarding/:packId/:scopeDirName`, `POST /api/packs/deploy`) are used directly in filesystem path construction (`join(baseDir, 'industry-pack-' + packId)`, `join(packDir, 'scopes', scopeDirName)`) without any traversal or containment check. An authenticated user can supply traversal sequences (e.g. `a/../../` and `../`-chains) to make the service read files outside the `industry-packs` directory tree and return their contents."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The `packId` and `scopeDirName` parameters of the pack-deploy endpoints (`GET /api/packs/onboarding/:packId/:scopeDirName`, `POST /api/packs/deploy`) are used directly in filesystem path construction (`join(baseDir, 'industry-pack-' + packId)`, `join(packDir, 'scopes', scopeDirName)`) without any traversal or containment check. An authenticated user can supply traversal sequences (e.g. `a/../../` and `../`-chains) to make the service read files outside the `industry-packs` directory tree and return their contents.\n\nRead-only local file disclosure: an authenticated attacker can read the contents of any file with the fixed names consumed by the service (`onboarding.json`, `scope.json`, `sop.md`, `SKILL.md`, `initial-memories.json`, `workflow-plan.json`, `manifest.json`) located anywhere on the server filesystem reachable by the backend process. No write or execute primitive was demonstrated: the appended filenames are fixed, so arbitrary-name files (e.g. `.env`, `package.json`, SSH keys) cannot be read directly, and all writes go through mocked/DB paths that only copy already-read content.\n\nValidate that `packId` and `scopeDirName` match a strict allowlist pattern (e.g. `^[A-Za-z0-9_-]+$`, no slashes or dots) before any path construction. Independently, after resolving the pack and scope directories, canonicalize with `resolve()` and verify the result starts with the canonical packs base directory plus path separator, rejecting otherwise. Apply the same check to the `deploy` flow, which shares the identical join pattern and additionally reads `scope.json`, agents, skills, SOP, and workflow files from the traversed directory.",
                "markdown": "The `packId` and `scopeDirName` parameters of the pack-deploy endpoints (`GET /api/packs/onboarding/:packId/:scopeDirName`, `POST /api/packs/deploy`) are used directly in filesystem path construction (`join(baseDir, 'industry-pack-' + packId)`, `join(packDir, 'scopes', scopeDirName)`) without any traversal or containment check. An authenticated user can supply traversal sequences (e.g. `a/../../` and `../`-chains) to make the service read files outside the `industry-packs` directory tree and return their contents.\n\nRead-only local file disclosure: an authenticated attacker can read the contents of any file with the fixed names consumed by the service (`onboarding.json`, `scope.json`, `sop.md`, `SKILL.md`, `initial-memories.json`, `workflow-plan.json`, `manifest.json`) located anywhere on the server filesystem reachable by the backend process. No write or execute primitive was demonstrated: the appended filenames are fixed, so arbitrary-name files (e.g. `.env`, `package.json`, SSH keys) cannot be read directly, and all writes go through mocked/DB paths that only copy already-read content.\n\nValidate that `packId` and `scopeDirName` match a strict allowlist pattern (e.g. `^[A-Za-z0-9_-]+$`, no slashes or dots) before any path construction. Independently, after resolving the pack and scope directories, canonicalize with `resolve()` and verify the result starts with the canonical packs base directory plus path separator, rejecting otherwise. Apply the same check to the `deploy` flow, which shares the identical join pattern and additionally reads `scope.json`, agents, skills, SOP, and workflow files from the traversed directory."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-22",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/22.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-639",
              "name": "Cross-organization IDOR in knowledge-base routes (read/modify/delete of other orgs' KBs, files, folders)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Cross-organization IDOR in knowledge-base routes (read/modify/delete of other orgs' KBs, files, folders)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Knowledge-base routes accept bare resource UUIDs and pass them to service methods that filter only by primary key, with no organization_id constraint. An authenticated member of any organization can read, rename, move, tag, or soft/hard-delete knowledge bases, folders, and files belonging to other organizations."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Knowledge-base routes accept bare resource UUIDs and pass them to service methods that filter only by primary key, with no organization_id constraint. An authenticated member of any organization can read, rename, move, tag, or soft/hard-delete knowledge bases, folders, and files belonging to other organizations.\n\nCross-tenant disclosure and destruction of another organization's knowledge-base content: file names, tags, sizes and metadata are returned by listFiles; display names/tags/folder assignments can be rewritten (updateFile, batchMoveFiles, batchAddTags); files can be hard-deleted (deleteFile, batchDeleteFiles); entire knowledge bases can be renamed or soft-deleted (update, delete). PoC: 9/9 assertions green.\n\nAdd organization_id derived from request.user.orgId to every where clause on these paths: change findById/update/delete to filter by {id, organization_id} (findFirst), filter listFiles by organization_id alongside knowledge_base_id, filter updateFile/deleteFile/batchMoveFiles/batchDeleteFiles/batchAddTags/updateStats, and validate that knowledge_base_id-scoped routes (folders, bindings, tags) first load the KB scoped to the caller's org. The only org-scoped method today is listByOrganization.",
                "markdown": "Knowledge-base routes accept bare resource UUIDs and pass them to service methods that filter only by primary key, with no organization_id constraint. An authenticated member of any organization can read, rename, move, tag, or soft/hard-delete knowledge bases, folders, and files belonging to other organizations.\n\nCross-tenant disclosure and destruction of another organization's knowledge-base content: file names, tags, sizes and metadata are returned by listFiles; display names/tags/folder assignments can be rewritten (updateFile, batchMoveFiles, batchAddTags); files can be hard-deleted (deleteFile, batchDeleteFiles); entire knowledge bases can be renamed or soft-deleted (update, delete). PoC: 9/9 assertions green.\n\nAdd organization_id derived from request.user.orgId to every where clause on these paths: change findById/update/delete to filter by {id, organization_id} (findFirst), filter listFiles by organization_id alongside knowledge_base_id, filter updateFile/deleteFile/batchMoveFiles/batchDeleteFiles/batchAddTags/updateStats, and validate that knowledge_base_id-scoped routes (folders, bindings, tags) first load the KB scoped to the caller's org. The only org-scoped method today is listByOrganization."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.8",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-639",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "vuln-0005",
              "name": "Missing authentication on avatar generation endpoints (POST /api/avatars/generate, /generate-batch)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Missing authentication on avatar generation endpoints (POST /api/avatars/generate, /generate-batch)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "POST /api/avatars/generate and POST /api/avatars/generate-batch lack the `authenticate` preHandler used by sibling avatar routes, so anonymous callers can invoke paid Nova Canvas image generation and write objects to the shared avatars S3 bucket when the feature flag is enabled."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "POST /api/avatars/generate and POST /api/avatars/generate-batch lack the `authenticate` preHandler used by sibling avatar routes, so anonymous callers can invoke paid Nova Canvas image generation and write objects to the shared avatars S3 bucket when the feature flag is enabled.\n\nUnauthenticated resource consumption (AWS Bedrock Nova Canvas per-image cost) and unauthenticated writes to the global avatars/ S3 namespace. Data confidentiality is unaffected (generated keys are returned only to the caller); impact is cost abuse plus unauthenticated state change.\n\nAdd the `preHandler: [authenticate]` option to the POST /avatars/generate and POST /avatars/generate-batch route registrations in avatarRoutes.ts, matching the /avatars/upload route. Optionally add rate limiting and per-user quota for avatar generation.",
                "markdown": "POST /api/avatars/generate and POST /api/avatars/generate-batch lack the `authenticate` preHandler used by sibling avatar routes, so anonymous callers can invoke paid Nova Canvas image generation and write objects to the shared avatars S3 bucket when the feature flag is enabled.\n\nUnauthenticated resource consumption (AWS Bedrock Nova Canvas per-image cost) and unauthenticated writes to the global avatars/ S3 namespace. Data confidentiality is unaffected (generated keys are returned only to the caller); impact is cost abuse plus unauthenticated state change.\n\nAdd the `preHandler: [authenticate]` option to the POST /avatars/generate and POST /avatars/generate-batch route registrations in avatarRoutes.ts, matching the /avatars/upload route. Optionally add rate limiting and per-user quota for avatar generation."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
