        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-22",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Path traversal in workspace provisioning via unsanitized skill/agent names (workspace-manager.ts)\n\nSkill and agent names are stored and later used in filesystem path joins (workspace-manager.ts) without any sanitization or containment check. An authenticated org user who creates a skill or agent with a `../`-containing name causes the backend, when provisioning a chat session workspace, to write files (SKILL.md, agent .md, or a copied skill directory) to attacker-chosen locations outside the intended `.claude/skills` and `.claude/agents` directories on the server."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "super-agent backend (local repository)",
                  "kind": "resource"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "3ed788d0220b3a2e7204cfd65c753faffe83e7ee4cca63df3bf1dc0042f0b63e"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "7.6",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "6c58275b746a212b66e13227aff805df9d5241b3a0ee6715ddb4941ae4556cfd",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0004",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 7.6,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-20 13:29:55 UTC",
              "target": "super-agent backend (local repository)",
              "cwe": "CWE-22",
              "impact": "An authenticated org user can make the backend write arbitrary-content files (SKILL.md content / agent markdown with a controlled system prompt, or a full copied skill directory) to arbitrary relative paths reachable from the workspace base directory — e.g. overwriting application configs, other tenants' workspaces, cron/system files writable by the service user, or planting files that a later interpreter loads. File content includes substantial attacker-controlled text (skill body/description, agent systemPrompt). Demonstrated locally with synthetic markers reading the written files back outside the skills/agents roots.",
              "technical_analysis": "Root cause is the absence of both input validation at creation time and containment checks at write time.\n\n**Unvalidated creation path.** `POST /api/skills` (skills.routes.ts:50-54) accepts `{ name, display_name, ... }` with only a TypeScript interface — no zod/JSON-schema validation on `name`. `skillService.createSkill` (skill.service.ts:77-89) stores `input.name` verbatim to Postgres (`skills.name VARCHAR(255)`, no format constraint in prisma/schema.prisma:265). Similarly `createAgentSchema` (agent.schema.ts:17-21) enforces only min/max length and non-whitespace — `../../evil` passes. Notably, agent and skill names flow into `ScopeForWorkspace` (chat.service.ts) unmodified.\n\n**Missing containment at the sink.** `WorkspaceManager.downloadSkill` (workspace-manager.ts:968-1044) computes `const skillDir = join(targetDir, skill.name)` with no containment check — unlike `readWorkspaceFile` (workspace-manager.ts:1474-1476), which explicitly rejects paths escaping `workspacePath`. The same unchecked join pattern exists in `generateAgentSubagentFiles` (workspace-manager.ts:756, `join(skillsDir, skill.name)` and :785 `${agent.name}.md`), `installSkillToWorkspace` (workspace-manager.ts:1359-1362, `join(skillsDir, skillName)` + `cp -r`), and `syncSkills` (workspace-manager.ts:862-883, removes/creates skill dirs by name). A `../` sequence in a name escapes the per-session workspace entirely: `join(skillsDir, '../../../../../../x')` resolves above the workspace base directory.\n\nThese sinks run during session provisioning (`ensureSessionWorkspace`, workspace-manager.ts:250-307), triggered whenever a chat session is created in a scope whose agents reference the crafted skill, and during workspace refresh on skill sync. Contrast: `createDocGroupSymlinks` (workspace-manager.ts:278) sanitizes document-group names with `replace(/[/\\\\:*?\"<>|]/g, '-')` — the codebase sanitizes this same class of user data elsewhere but not for skills/agents.",
              "remediation_steps": "Enforce a strict name format at the API boundary: validate skill and agent names with a schema (e.g. `^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*$`, no `/`, `\\`, or leading dots) in the create/update routes and services, and reject existing stored names that violate it before workspace provisioning. Defense in depth: in WorkspaceManager, apply the same containment check used by `readWorkspaceFile` (resolve the joined path and verify it starts with the intended skills/agents directory) in `downloadSkill`, `generateAgentSubagentFiles`, `installSkillToWorkspace`, and when syncing/removing stale skill directories.",
              "counterevidence": "Actively searched for controls: (1) skills.routes.ts has no JSON-schema or zod validation on POST / body name — only TypeScript interfaces; skill.service.ts createSkill passes input.name to the repository verbatim; updateSkill cannot change name but create can set any value. (2) The Prisma schema (skills.name VARCHAR(255)) and migration impose no format constraint — slashes and dots are valid characters. (3) The contrast control readWorkspaceFile at workspace-manager.ts:1474-1476 explicitly implements prefix containment, showing the codebase knows this pattern but did not apply it to skill/agent name joins. (4) Notably, document-group symlink names ARE sanitized with .replace(/[/\\\\:*?\"<>|]/g, '-') (workspace-manager.ts:278), another proof sanitization exists elsewhere but not for skill/agent names. (5) Scope-generator-created skills and marketplace skills also feed these paths; I did not audit every name source, but the primary user-controlled route (POST /api/skills) is unvalidated, which alone establishes reachability. Searched for sanitization between service and workspace-manager (scope building in chat.service.ts/agent-runtime-claude.ts) — names are passed through as-is.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Demonstrating that session workspaces are provisioned with the process owner able to write to a path that influences execution (e.g. another tenant's workspace, a service config, or a writable autoload directory) would raise confidentiality/availability and potentially the severity to high; if a name-format validation exists in an ingest layer not examined here (e.g. enterprise skill publish or import), reachability of the primary POST /api/skills route would need re-checking.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Create a skill via POST /api/skills with name `../../../../../../escaped-skill` and a body/description (no route/service validation exists). 2. Attach the skill to a business scope/agent so it enters SkillForWorkspace. 3. Start a chat session; ensureSessionWorkspace calls downloadSkill which computes join(skillsDir, skill.name) and writes SKILL.md there. 4. Observe the marker file written outside the skills directory. Repeat with an agent named `../../../../../evil-agent` (generateAgentSubagentFiles writes `${agent.name}.md` into agentsDir) and with installSkillToWorkspace (skillName joined into skillsDir then cp -r).",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
